  ### [good] How close one request gets to the hardware limit (B01)
  
  **One request runs at 81% of the GPU's memory bandwidth (5.9 ms per token against a 4.8 ms floor): healthy.**
  
  **What we measured.** With nothing else running, the median TPOT (time per output token, the gap between words once the answer is streaming) across 30 identical requests was 5.9 ms. The floor for this model on NVIDIA H100 SXM 80GB is 4.8 ms, so decoding reaches 81% MBU (model bandwidth utilization, how much of the GPU's memory speed decoding actually uses).
  
  **What it means.** The engine is doing its job: there is nothing to fix for single-request speed. The sweeps measure how this changes as users are added. Like a car on an empty highway: if it can't get near the speed limit, the problem is the car, not the traffic.
  
  **Why it happens.** With one request, each decode step is almost entirely reading weights from memory, so speed is bounded by bandwidth. Well-tuned engines reach 60 to 80% of it; Databricks reported about 60% at batch 1 on two H100s. The gap is launch overhead and small operations that can't use the full bandwidth. [Learn more: Lesson 24: Establish the single-request baseline, Compare against the floors](https://github.com/S1LV3RJ1NX/inference-engineering-notes/blob/main/notes/24-establish-single-request-baseline.md#4-compare-against-the-floors)
  
  ### [good] Consistency between identical requests (B04)
  
  **Time per token was steady across identical requests (variation 7.0%, P99 1.3× the median).**
  
  **What we measured.** 30 identical requests: median 5.9 ms, P99 (99 out of 100 requests were at least this fast; the slow tail users notice) 7.5 ms, coefficient of variation 7.0%.
  
  **What it means.** The measurement is repeatable, so differences in later runs are real.
  
  **Why it happens.** Every baseline request is identical, so they should take the same time. Spread means something else is involved: another workload on the GPU, throttling, or a noisy network path. [Learn more: Lesson 24: Establish the single-request baseline, When it looks off](https://github.com/S1LV3RJ1NX/inference-engineering-notes/blob/main/notes/24-establish-single-request-baseline.md#5-when-it-looks-off)
  
  ### [context] The fastest this setup can possibly go (T01)
  
  **Physics floor for one request (512 in, 256 out): 4.8 ms per token, 1.23 s end to end.**
  
  **What we measured.** Before sending any traffic, kneebench worked out the floor (the fastest time physics allows on this hardware; nothing can beat it) from the model's size and the published speeds of NVIDIA H100 SXM 80GB. Reading all 16.1 GB of weights once takes 4.8 ms at 3.35 TB/s, so no token can arrive faster. Reading a 512-token prompt needs at least 8.3 ms of math at 989 TFLOPS.
  
  | Shape | Prompt tokens | Output tokens | TTFT floor | TPOT floor | End-to-end floor |
  |---|---|---|---|---|---|
  | baseline | 512 | 256 | 8.3 ms | 4.8 ms | 1.23 s |
  | chat | 1,024 | 1,024 | 16.6 ms | 4.8 ms | 4.92 s |
  | reasoning | 1,024 | 7,040 | 16.6 ms | 4.8 ms | 33.8 s |
  | summarization | 7,040 | 1,024 | 114 ms | 4.8 ms | 5.02 s |
  
  **What it means.** This is the ruler every later number is measured against. Real servers always land above it; how far above tells you whether something is misconfigured.
  
  **Why it happens.** Writing the answer is called decode (the word-by-word step that writes the answer, limited by GPU memory speed): each step reads every weight from GPU memory once, so time per token is at least weights divided by memory bandwidth. Reading the prompt is called prefill (the step that reads the whole prompt at once, limited by GPU math): it does about two math operations per parameter per prompt token, so it is bounded by the GPU's math throughput. End to end is the first token plus one decode step for each remaining token. [Learn more: Lesson 24: Establish the single-request baseline, Work out the floors first](https://github.com/S1LV3RJ1NX/inference-engineering-notes/blob/main/notes/24-establish-single-request-baseline.md#2-work-out-the-floors-first)
  
  ### [context] How many requests fit in memory at once (T03)
  
  **The cache holds 532,768 tokens: baseline about 694 at full length, chat about 260 at full length, reasoning about 66 at full length, summarization about 66 at full length.**
  
  **What we measured.** The cache size divided by each shape's final length (prompt plus output).
  
  | Shape | Prompt tokens | Output tokens | Requests that fit |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | baseline | 512 | 256 | 694 |
  | chat | 1,024 | 1,024 | 260 |
  | reasoning | 1,024 | 7,040 | 66 |
  | summarization | 7,040 | 1,024 | 66 |
  
  **What it means.** This is the memory ceiling on how many of these requests can be in flight together. Past it, the server either queues new requests or evicts running ones.
  
  **Why it happens.** A request holds memory for its prompt plus everything it has generated so far, so its final length is what counts, not its starting length. Long answers start small and grow, which is why reasoning workloads run out of memory later than they first appear to. [Learn more: Lesson 26: Sweep prompt and output length, Shape decides how many fit](https://github.com/S1LV3RJ1NX/inference-engineering-notes/blob/main/notes/26-sweep-prompt-output-length.md#4-shape-decides-how-many-fit)
  
  ### [context] How much memory each token of context takes (T02)
  
  **Each token of context needs 128 KiB of KV cache (the memory where the server keeps each request's context while it answers); the server reserved room for 532,768 tokens (65.0 GiB per GPU).**
  
  **What we measured.** From the model's architecture: 128 KiB per token. From the server's /metrics: 532,768 tokens of cache after reserving 90% of GPU memory and loading the weights.
  
  **What it means.** Every request in flight holds this much memory per token of its prompt and answer. The pool is the total budget all users share.
  
  **Why it happens.** For every token in a conversation, each layer keeps a key and a value vector so later tokens can look back at it: bytes per token = 2 x layers x KV heads x head size x bytes per number. vLLM reserves a share of GPU memory, loads the weights, and gives what's left to this cache. Every decode step rereads the cache of every token so far, which is why long prompts also slow decode. [Learn more: Lesson 26: Sweep prompt and output length, The prompt changes decode too](https://github.com/S1LV3RJ1NX/inference-engineering-notes/blob/main/notes/26-sweep-prompt-output-length.md#2-sweep-the-prompt-output-fixed-at-256)
  
  ### [context] Shapes adjusted to fit the context window (T05)
  
  **reasoning 1,024/8,192 became 1,024/7,040; summarization 8,192/1,024 became 7,040/1,024 to fit the 8,192-token context.**
  
  **What we measured.** Prompt plus output plus room for the chat template must fit in 8,192 tokens.
  
  **What it means.** kneebench shortened the longer side of these shapes. Their results describe the adjusted lengths, not the originals.
  
  **Why it happens.** A request can't be longer than the server's context window, so a shape that doesn't fit would fail every request instead of measuring anything. [Learn more: Lesson 26: Sweep prompt and output length, Running the sweep](https://github.com/S1LV3RJ1NX/inference-engineering-notes/blob/main/notes/26-sweep-prompt-output-length.md#5-running-the-sweep)
  
  **What to do.** Raise the context limit if your real traffic is longer. Try `--max-model-len <tokens>`. Expected: The original shapes can be measured as-is. Verify: Rerun; this note disappears.
  
  ## Checks that passed
  
  - [good] **Did every answer reach the requested length?** (H04): Every point generated the full requested output length (1 checked).
  - [good] **Versions under test** (H07): vLLM 0.29.0 is within the tested range (0.29.0 up to 0.33.0). Recorded: kneebench 0.0.test, AIPerf 0.13.0.
  - [good] **Room for a full-length request** (T04): The cache holds 65× full-length (8,192-token) requests: room to spare.
  - [good] **Measured speed versus the claimed GPU** (T06): Measured 5.9 ms per token is consistent with NVIDIA H100 SXM 80GB.
  
  ## Not checked
  
  - **Time to first token versus its floor** (B03): Server-side time to first token can't be separated from network time from this location (see "Distance between kneebench and the server").
  
  ## What this test can't see
  
  - kneebench only sees the endpoint. It has no GPU telemetry; memory and queue signals come from vLLM's /metrics.
  - Prompts are random text, so they never share a beginning. Real traffic often repeats system prompts, which prefix caching can speed up; your real time to first token may be lower.
  - Results describe this server, with these flags, at this time. Change one setting at a time and rerun to compare.
  - This test ran 272 ms (one network round trip) away from the server. Time per token is reliable from here; first-token times include the network.
  - The server has prefix caching (reusing work for prompts that start with the same text) on; this test's prompts hit it 5.9% of the time. Real traffic that repeats system prompts will likely start answers faster than measured here.
  
  ## Glossary
  
  - **KV cache**: the memory where the server keeps each request's context while it answers
  - **MBU**: model bandwidth utilization, how much of the GPU's memory speed decoding actually uses
  - **P99**: 99 out of 100 requests were at least this fast; the slow tail users notice
  - **TPOT**: time per output token, the gap between words once the answer is streaming
  - **TTFT**: time to first token, how long a user waits before the answer starts appearing
  - **decode**: the word-by-word step that writes the answer, limited by GPU memory speed
  - **floor**: the fastest time physics allows on this hardware; nothing can beat it
  - **prefill**: the step that reads the whole prompt at once, limited by GPU math
  - **prefix caching**: reusing work for prompts that start with the same text
  
  ## Appendix: raw numbers
  
  ### Measured points
  
  | # | Phase | Status | Prompt tokens | Output tokens | Concurrency | Requests | TTFT P50 | TTFT P99 | TPOT P50 | TPOT P99 | E2E P50 | Output tok/s | Error % |
  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
  | 0 | baseline | done | 512 | 256 | 1 | 30 | 306 ms | 346 ms | 5.9 ms | 7.5 ms | 1.8 s | 138.2 | 0.0 |
  
  ### Client distance
  
  | DNS | TCP connect | TLS handshake | HTTP round trip (/health) |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | 2.6 ms | 268 ms | 269 ms | 272 ms |
  
  ### Setup
  
  | Item | Value |
  |---|---|
  | Model | unsloth/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct |
  | Parameters | 8,030,261,248 |
  | Weight bytes | 16,060,522,496 |
  | Weight dtype | bfloat16 |
  | KV bytes per token | 131,072 |
  | GPU | 1× NVIDIA H100 SXM 80GB |
  | GPU memory (GiB) | 79.6 |
  | GPU bandwidth (TB/s) | 3.35 |
  | KV cache pool (tokens) | 532,768 |
  | max_model_len | 8,192 |
  | gpu_memory_utilization | 0.90 |
  | Default max_num_seqs for this vLLM and GPU | 1,024 |
  | vLLM | 0.29.0 |
  | AIPerf | 0.13.0 |
  
  
  _Versions: kneebench_version 0.1.0.dev0, aiperf_version 0.13.0, rules_version 1._
  
  '''
